title The guard that could not see its own shelf
prompt Baton lap -- core infrastructure, Lindy-first crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4143, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; the Fixed seat (the happy zone and the thin edge) named the shape this lap needed: a seam carries ONE contract, and two small families of checks mirror it from each side, which turns a multiplication of paths into an addition. The scope map is exactly such a seam, and its contract -- does this watch-set reach this path -- was about to be written twice.
think Opened with the roster cold pass in the background and read the card whole while it ran.
think The cold pass reddened at index_row_bound: rows_misordered=1 on today's shelf, 20260907.021117 seated above 20260907.023053. Standfast -- the red books the lap. Swapped the two rows by hand and the scan returned verdict=ok.
think That is the TWELFTH firing of %440 across five laps, every one repaired by the same hand-swap. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom, so the lap became: build the loom, then find why the fault reaches a cold open at all.
think Wrote tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh. It asks tools/fixtures/i/index_row_bound_scan.sh which shelf is open and what is wrong with it -- rather than deriving the open shelf a second time -- so it can never repair a page the guard is not reading. It sorts the contiguous run of table rows below the delimiter with LC_ALL=C sort -r, which is chronological-descending for rows whose first cell is a fixed-width stamp, and refuses a row shaped otherwise.
think The postcondition is what makes an unattended rewrite of testimony safe: the sorted line multiset must equal the original's, compared before a byte is written. Refuses on duplicate stamps (which of two rows to lift is a hand's judgment), on an unstamped row, and on a page with no delimiter -- each leaving the file byte-identical.
think The scan's refusal now prints advice=sh tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh, so a number comes with a way to act.
think Then the root. DIFFUSER's paper named a buildable item for this seat -- rank the scope map by cost x (1 - touch_rate), both factors on disk. Building it meant reading tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_scope_map.sh, and reading it meant answering the same question the runner answers: does this watch-set reach this path.
think Rather than spell that twice, extracted it once into tools/fixtures/s/scope_match.sh -- scope_match_word, scope_match_row, scope_match_any -- and had tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_run.sh source it beside shell_portable.sh, replacing its inline case loop. The runner's own control proves every scoped leg still green.
think With the matcher in hand, asked it whether index_row_bound's map row reaches session-logs/date/README-index-20260907.md. It answered no. The row named four paths and none was under session-logs/date/, so a --scoped pass SKIPPED the only guard that reads the open shelf -- which is why %440 keeps being found at a later ship's cold open rather than by the lap that wrote the row.
think That is the map header's own named hazard, a static row naming less than its guard gates -- thirteen repaired by a hand on 20260906, and this is the fourteenth. Booked as REDS 20260907.045528, CLOSED, and made permanent as a third gate: rows_missing_control, which holds every static row to reaching its guard's own <name>_control.sh. Measured before adding: 26 rows with such a control, all reaching, 30 guards with no file at that name (counted apart, gated on nothing, since a naming convention is not a law).
obs The load-bearing leg of the ranking control is a NONZERO touch rate. The first draft used one awk name as both an array and a scalar; awk refused the row it reached AFTER every summary line had printed, so every rate read 0.000 and every row appeared to save its whole cost, against a table that looked perfectly healthy. A quietly-zero meter passes every welcome leg ever written for it, so the control also runs a copy with the reach pass stripped and asserts it reads exactly that.
obs A cost nobody measured reads unknown rather than zero, stays out of every sum and out of the ranking. On this pier that is 56 guards -- the entire cadence tier has never recorded a run here -- so a printed zero would have read as "costs nothing" for the most expensive guards in the tree.
obs Two methods agree independently: this scan reads 80.1% of known cost unmapped where DIFFUSER's paper read 80.2% by a different route.
obs My own fault, named: I edited standing_equipment_run.sh while its cold pass was reading it, and the running shell died with a syntax error at line 934 -- REDS %221 exactly, whose own header warns of it. The file is sound; the pass is not. The hot pass over the staged tree is the green that stands.
obs A second fault of my own, caught by an existing control: adding the source line to the runner broke standing_equipment_control.sh's blind-runner pen, which copies the runner into a pen and had copied only shell_portable.sh beside it. Every reading there answered no for a reason that had nothing to do with the phase. Fixed by copying scope_match.sh too, and the reason is written where the copy happens.
obs The map has a word for a guard deliberately left unmapped -- the runner honors DISCOVERY -- and no row uses it, so all 163 unmapped guards read absent and a hand cannot tell a census from a gap. Named on the card rather than taken: writing 163 rows is a survey, not a lap.
